round it (GitHub access, connectors, the environment's secrets, network access, setup script and installed tools, the session's limits and permission prompts, Remote Control). Read it whenever something about your container or environment comes up, whether it blocked you, you worked around it, or the person asked how to set it up. For example: the repository the work is about is not in your container, a clone or push is refused, a service you need has no connected connector, an outbound host is denied, a command-line tool is missing, you need an API key, the person asks how to stop permission prompts. Read it rather than answering from memory because these settings move and get renamed faster than your training data, and a page says what is true now: the current steps, where in the product the person makes the change, and what you can do yourself. Reading a page also helps the person directly: in the Claude Code app they see a card with a button that takes them to that settings page, so they can fix it themselves while you carry on. Call it with no topic to list the pages and when each applies; call it with a topic to read that page. Read a page each time a different topic comes up; one read per topic is enough. It is read-only and needs no approval. It has nothing on bugs in the code you are working on.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__read_documentation", "schema": {"description": "The documentation for the machine and product this session runs in: a claude.ai cloud container and the settings around it (GitHub access, connectors, the environment's secrets, network access, setup script and installed tools, the session's limits and permission prompts, Remote Control). Read it whenever something about your container or environment comes up, whether it blocked you, you worked around it, or the person asked how to set it up. For example: the repository the work is about is not in your container, a clone or push is refused, a service you need has no connected connector, an outbound host is denied, a command-line tool is missing, you need an API key, the person asks how to stop permission prompts. Read it rather than answering from memory because these settings move and get renamed faster than your training data, and a page says what is true now: the current steps, where in the product the person makes the change, and what you can do yourself. Reading a page also helps the person directly: in the Claude Code app they see a card with a button that takes them to that settings page, so they can fix it themselves while you carry on. Call it with no topic to list the pages and when each applies; call it with a topic to read that page. Read a page each time a different topic comes up; one read per topic is enough. It is read-only and needs no approval. It has nothing on bugs in the code you are working on.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"situation": {"description": "Why you are reading the page. \"blocked\": you cannot finish what was asked. \"worked_around\": you finished another way. \"asked\": the person asked how to set this up, or you can see it will be needed.", "enum": ["blocked", "worked_around", "asked"], "type": "string"}, "topic": {"description": "The page to read. Must be one of this field's enum values. Omit it to get the index of pages.", "enum": ["github.access", "connectors.add", "connectors.tool_off", "environment.secrets", "environment.network", "environment.dependencies", "environment.setup_script", "session.resources", "session.permissions", "remote_control.setup"], "type": "string"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__read_documentation"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Tell the session that a repo attached via add_repo has finished cloning, so its CLAUDE.md, skills, and plugins load on the next turn. Only call this immediately after a successful clone that add_repo instructed you to run — it returns a tool error for a repo that is not already in this session's sources.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__register_repo_root", "schema": {"description": "Tell the session that a repo attached via add_repo has finished cloning, so its CLAUDE.md, skills, and plugins load on the next turn. Only call this immediately after a successful clone that add_repo instructed you to run — it returns a tool error for a repo that is not already in this session's sources.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"directory": {"description": "Absolute path of the clone on disk. Pass the real path you cloned to; on a self-hosted runner this will be under the session's base working directory.", "type": "string"}, "owner": {"description": "GitHub owner of the repo that was just cloned (same value passed to add_repo).", "type": "string"}, "repo": {"description": "GitHub repo name that was just cloned (same value passed to add_repo).", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["owner", "repo"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__register_repo_root"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Tell the person's app which port on this session's machine serves a web page they may want to look at. The person may be following this session from a phone or a browser, where a preview pane can show that page. The app does not know the port unless you say so.\n\n- Call this when a dev server or other web server is up and the person may want to see its page, or when you are about to point them to one that was already running. Give the port it really listens on: read it from the server's output, not from what you asked for, since many servers move to the next free port.\n- Call it once for each server, and again only if that server's port changes. A restart on the same port needs no new call.\n- When more than one server runs, give each a short name, such as \"web\" or \"docs\".\n- Do not call it for a database, a debugger, an API with no page, a port nothing listens on, or a server you start only for your own tests.\n- This tool only states the port: it starts nothing and opens nothing. The app decides what to show and the person decides whether to open it. Not every app shows a preview, so do not tell the person one is ready because you called this.\n- If a tool whose name ends in preview_start started the server, that call already gave the port, so do not call this tool for that server.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__report_preview_port", "schema": {"description": "Tell the person's app which port on this session's machine serves a web page they may want to look at. The person may be following this session from a phone or a browser, where a preview pane can show that page. The app does not know the port unless you say so.\n\n- Call this when a dev server or other web server is up and the person may want to see its page, or when you are about to point them to one that was already running. Give the port it really listens on: read it from the server's output, not from what you asked for, since many servers move to the next free port.\n- Call it once for each server, and again only if that server's port changes. A restart on the same port needs no new call.\n- When more than one server runs, give each a short name, such as \"web\" or \"docs\".\n- Do not call it for a database, a debugger, an API with no page, a port nothing listens on, or a server you start only for your own tests.\n- This tool only states the port: it starts nothing and opens nothing. The app decides what to show and the person decides whether to open it. Not every app shows a preview, so do not tell the person one is ready because you called this.\n- If a tool whose name ends in preview_start started the server, that call already gave the port, so do not call this tool for that server.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"name": {"description": "A short name for the server, such as \"web\", when more than one runs. 